Negative control for the page keeping its FULL WIDTH while a sheet is open
(T3.2 · AC3.1 · P1-R5).

The sheet is `:modal="false"` and overlays the canvas, so the page must not know
it is there. The regression this pins is the one every drawer picks up sooner or
later: somebody decides the panel is "covering the content" and pushes the page
out of its way, which is the reflow `P1-R5` exists to forbid — and it is invisible
to any assertion that only checks the panel rendered, or that it teleported out
of the page's own tree, because both stay true with the page squeezed.

This mutant leaves the host, the toggle, the url state and the panel exactly as
they are. It only makes the LAYOUT compensate: the main column gains right
padding the width of the panel while a sheet is open, and reads the host's open
state to do it.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the T3.2 case asserting the page's own content column carries the same
classes with a sheet open as with none — no width, no padding compensation
anywhere above the host.

  Verified 2026-08-12 (`vitest run --project component sheet-host.spec.ts`).
  First run under this mutant: 13 passed — the control caught the spec, not the
  layout. The two source-reading cases tokenised a BOUND attribute as one string,
  so `:class="['pt-4', isOpen ? 'pr-96' : '']"` hid both the compensation and the
  read of the host's open state inside it. Spec strengthened (bound expressions
  tokenised down to their literals; the sheet-state read derived from the
  layout's own `usePlaygroundSheet()` destructure rather than the word "sheet").
  Re-run: 2 failed | 11 passed —
    reserves no room for the sheet in the layout that mounts it
        -> expected [ 'pr-96' ] to deeply equal []
    binds no layout treatment to whether a sheet is open — nothing reflows
        -> expected [ "['pt-4', isOpen ? 'pr-96' : '']" ] to deeply equal []
  `git apply -R` restores 13 passed.

